.\" bluetoothd(8) — NextBSD Bluetooth daemon.
.\"
.\" NextBSD-original. There is no upstream page to vendor: Apple's bluetoothd
.\" is closed source and undocumented.
.\"
.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt BLUETOOTHD 8
.Os NextBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm bluetoothd
.Nd Bluetooth daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl d
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
owns Bluetooth on NextBSD.
It is started by
.Xr launchd 8
as
.Ql org.nextbsd.bluetoothd ,
serves the Mach service
.Ql org.nextbsd.bluetooth ,
and is not intended to be run by hand.
Use
.Xr bluetooth 8
to talk to it.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl d
Also log debug-level lines, such as why a USB device was not handed to the
firmware loader.
.El
.Pp
.Nm
and
.Xr bluetooth 8
are the only Bluetooth programs on the system.
The kernel stack, loaded as kexts, carries HCI and L2CAP; everything above it
lives in
.Nm :
controller bring-up and power, discovery, pairing for Classic (BR/EDR) and LE
with one pairing store, the SDP server and client, the HID host and the audio
roles.
It runs as root from boot, so a paired keyboard works at the login window.
.Pp
Bluetooth is on by default.
A controller is powered on when it arrives unless the persisted
.Ql ControllerPowerState
is 0, and paired devices reconnect without user action.
.Sh CONTROLLERS
.Pa NgUbt.kext
publishes each controller in the IORegistry as an
.Ql IOBluetoothHostController
entry once its netgraph node exists, and retires the entry when the controller
goes away.
.Nm
follows that class with
.Fn IOServiceAddMatchingNotification ,
first-match and terminated, as
.Xr wland 8
follows
.Ql IO80211Controller .
A controller that is already present when
.Nm
starts is delivered as an arrival.
.Pp
With no controller,
.Nm
logs one line, blocks with no timer and loads no kexts: the upper kernel stack
is loaded when the first controller arrives.
On a kernel without
.Pa /dev/ioregistry
no controller can be seen;
.Nm
stays up to answer
.Xr bluetooth 8
and reports itself as blind.
.Pp
When a controller arrives,
.Nm
brings it up:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
The first controller loads the upper kernel stack,
.Pa NgSocket.kext ,
.Pa NgL2cap.kext
and
.Pa NgBtsocket.kext ,
through
.Fn KextManagerLoadKextWithIdentifier .
.It
It builds the netgraph graph over the driver's node: an HCI node
.Pq Ql ubt0hci ,
an L2CAP node
.Pq Ql ubt0l2cap ,
and their hooks to the raw HCI, L2CAP and SCO socket nodes.
A graph that outlived a restart of
.Nm
is adopted as it is.
.It
It initialises the controller over a raw HCI socket: reset, address,
features, buffer sizes (Classic and LE), event masks, Secure Simple Pairing,
inquiry mode, class of device, and a local name taken from the computer name.
.It
It powers the controller on (page scan, and inquiry scan if discoverable),
unless
.Ql ControllerPowerState
says off.
.El
.Pp
When the controller goes away, its graph is shut down and its dynamic store
key removed.
.Sh FIRMWARE
Intel and Realtek controllers start in a boot loader and need firmware before
the driver attaches; until then they are only an
.Ql IOUSBHostDevice
in the IORegistry.
.Nm
watches those by vendor id, whether present at start or arriving later, and
for one with no driver downloads the firmware itself, from
.Pa /System/Library/Extensions/NgUbt.kext/Contents/Resources/firmware ,
then resets the device so that it comes back as a controller.
The download protocol is ported from the BSD-licensed Intel and Realtek
loaders; no separate program is run.
The outcome is logged, shown by
.Ql bluetooth status ,
and published as
.Ql State:/Bluetooth/Firmware/<ugen> .
.Sh ARCHITECTURE
.Bd -literal -offset indent
NgUbt.kext ──IOBluetoothHostController──▶ bluetoothd
bluetoothd ──raw HCI / L2CAP sockets──▶ kernel stack
           ──SCPreferences────────────▶ pairing store + policy
           ──SCDynamicStore───────────▶ State:/Bluetooth
           ──MIG org.nextbsd.bluetooth──▶ bluetooth(8) ──▶ Gershwin
.Ed
.Pp
Inside, a policy layer (controllers, the device list, pairing prompts, the
store, publication, the Mach service) sits above two protocol engines, Classic
and LE, behind one interface.
The policy layer never needs to know which transport a device uses: addresses
are typed (public or random), one prompt shape covers legacy PIN, Secure Simple
Pairing and LE pairing, and one bond record holds both engines' keys.
.Sh SERVICE DISCOVERY
.Nm
is the SDP server and client; no other program serves SDP, and there is no
.Pa /var/run/sdp
socket.
.Pp
The server listens on L2CAP PSM 1 on every controller and answers the
ServiceSearch, ServiceAttribute and ServiceSearchAttribute requests, with
continuation.
Its records live in memory and are registered by the part of
.Nm
that implements each service: the SDP server record and a PnP Information
(Device ID) record always; A2DP Sink and Source, AVRCP Target and Controller,
and the Hands-Free Audio Gateway only while that role is enabled.
.Pp
The client reads a device's records when it pairs: the HID record (the report
descriptor, ReconnectInitiate, NormallyConnectable, the parser version), the
PnP ID, and the service classes that become its
.Ql Profiles
.Pq HID, A2DPSink, A2DPSource, AVRCP, HFP .
They are kept in the pairing store, so a reconnect needs no service query.
.Ql bluetooth sdp Ar address
browses a device's records by hand.
.Sh PAIRING PROMPTS
Nothing above the Mach line can receive a Mach message, so
.Nm
never calls a client back.
When pairing needs the user,
.Nm
parks the request and a client fetches and answers it
.Pq the pull model .
A prompt is one of: confirm (just works), numeric comparison, passkey entry,
passkey display, or a legacy PIN.
It is published as
.Ql State:/Bluetooth/Pairing/<addr>
with a
.Ql org.nextbsd.bluetooth.pairing
notification, and expires after 60 seconds.
.Pp
Only pairings started here
.Pq Ql bluetooth pair
are accepted; a pairing a remote device starts is refused and logged.
A device with no input and no output, such as headphones, is accepted
without a question.
A paired device's link key is answered from the store, so it reconnects
after a restart; a new key is saved the moment the controller reports it.
.Sh LOW ENERGY
Keyboards and mice sold in recent years are mostly LE-only and use HID over
GATT.
The LE engine runs over the kernel's L2CAP sockets: one on the Attribute
Protocol channel (CID 4), whose connection makes the kernel create the LE
link, and one on the Security Manager channel (CID 6).
Scanning, encryption and the long-term key requests go over the raw HCI
socket.
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Ql bluetooth scan
scans LE actively alongside the inquiry; LE devices join the same list, a
random address with a
.Ql /random
suffix.
.It
.Ql bluetooth pair
picks the engine from what the scan saw: LE for random addresses and LE-only
devices, Classic for the rest, dual-mode devices included.
LE pairing offers Secure Connections and falls back to legacy pairing.
A mouse pairs with no question; a keyboard is given a passkey to type; a
device with a display shows a number to compare or a passkey to enter.
The device's long-term key, identity resolving key and signature key go to
the key file; a bond is kept under the identity address the device gives.
.It
While a paired LE device with AutoConnect is not connected,
.Nm
scans passively in the background and connects as soon as it advertises,
its private address resolved with its identity resolving key, before anyone
logs in.
The link is encrypted with the stored key before any other traffic.
.It
On an encrypted link to a device with the HID service,
.Nm
reads its report map and report references, enables notifications on its
input reports, and creates an
.Ql IOHIDUserDevice
(Transport
.Ql BluetoothLowEnergy )
that the kernel's HID drivers attach to.
Output reports, such as the keyboard LEDs, are written back.
The report map and PnP ID are cached in the device's
.Ql HIDDescriptor
and
.Ql PnPID ,
so a reconnect skips reading them.
.El
.Sh CONNECTIONS
.Nm
follows every Classic link: who connected, through which controller,
whether it is encrypted, and its signal (read every 10 seconds).
A new link to a paired device is authenticated with the stored key and then
encrypted.
The kernel accepts every incoming link; one from a device that is not paired
and
.Ql Trusted ,
and not being paired, is disconnected at once and logged.
.Pp
When a controller comes up powered, every paired device whose
.Ql AutoConnect
is on (and the global
.Ql AutoConnect )
is connected once, ten seconds apart, with no user action and whether or not
anyone is logged in.
A HID device is connected by the host only until its first HID session;
after that, a device whose SDP record says it reconnects by itself
.Pq Ql HIDReconnectInitiate
is left to page the computer, and
.Nm
only listens.
LE devices are reconnected by the LE engine itself (see
.Sx LOW ENERGY ) ;
their links are followed and published the same way.
.Sh HID HOST
.Nm
is the Classic HID host; no other program takes part.
It runs HIDP over the L2CAP control (PSM 0x11) and interrupt (PSM 0x13)
channels, as initiator for a device's first session after pairing and as a
listener on every controller for a device that reconnects itself.
.Pp
Each connected HID device becomes one
.Xr cuse 3 ,
.Xr IOHIDUserDevice 3 ,
built from the report descriptor cached at pairing time, with the device's
vendor and product from its PnP ID, transport
.Ql Bluetooth ,
its address as serial number and its name as product.
The kernel's HID drivers attach to it as to a USB device:
.Nm hkbd
for keyboards (through
.Nm kbdmux ,
so the console and the login window see it),
.Nm hms
and
.Nm hmt
for pointers.
Input reports go to the device unchanged; keyboard LEDs and other output
reports go back as HIDP DATA on the interrupt channel, feature reports as
SET_REPORT, and report reads as GET_REPORT.
A keyboard or mouse whose descriptor is not known is driven in boot
protocol.
.Pp
When the device disconnects, its
.Xr IOHIDUserDevice 3
is cancelled and the kernel releases any key still held down.
A device that unplugs its virtual cable is forgotten; unpairing one sends it
the same.
.Sh AUDIO
.Nm
is the only Bluetooth audio process; no FreeBSD Bluetooth helper is run.
.Ss A2DP source
When the link to a paired device whose services include an A2DP sink (a
headset or speaker) comes up, whichever side paged the other, and the
device's
.Ql Audio
switch is on,
.Nm
runs NextBSD's base mixer for it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
virtual_oss -S -C 2 -c 2 -r 48000 -b 16 -s 768 -R /dev/null \e
    -P /dev/bluetooth/AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF -d dsp.btN [-d dsp] \e
    -t dsp.btN.ctl
.Ed
.Pp
The mixer's Bluetooth backend,
.Pa /usr/lib/virtual_oss/voss_bt.so ,
finds the AVDTP channel, negotiates SBC and streams;
.Nm
owns the link and its keys.
Programs play to
.Pa /dev/dsp.btN .
With
.Ql AutoSwitchAudio
on, the first headset also takes
.Pa /dev/dsp ,
so programs that open the default device play to it; the system device
comes back when the headset goes.
.Pp
.Xr virtual_oss 8
never gives up on a sink that has gone: it retries every two seconds and
pages the headset each time.
So the mixer lives exactly as long as the link: it is stopped (SIGTERM,
then SIGKILL) when the link drops, the device is unpaired, its switch is
turned off or the controller goes, and restarted with a backoff only while
the link is up.
A mixer left behind by a crashed
.Nm
is stopped when the next one starts.
.Ss AVRCP
.Nm
listens on AVCTP's PSM 0x17 and opens the channel itself when a headset has
not.
As target it accepts the headset's play, pause, stop, next, previous,
volume, mute, fast-forward and rewind buttons and delivers them through an
.Xr IOHIDUserDevice 3
with a Consumer Control collection, so
.Nm hcons
attaches and they arrive as ordinary media keys.
As controller it follows the headset's absolute volume (AVRCP 1.4) and sets
it with
.Ql bluetooth audio Ar address Cm volume .
Other requests are answered NOT IMPLEMENTED, so the link stays up.
.Ss A2DP sink
With
.Ql A2DPSink
on, this machine is a Bluetooth speaker: it publishes the Audio Sink
service record, adds the Audio and Rendering bits to its Class of Device
and accepts AVDTP on PSM 0x19.
A phone's SBC stream is decoded in
.Nm
and played to
.Pa /dev/dsp ,
one stream at a time.
PSM 0x19 is shared with the source role: a device known to be a sink that
opens AVDTP is refused, and the mixer connects to it instead.
Off by default, since it advertises the machine to everything nearby.
.Ss Hands-free (HFP)
.Nm
is a Hands-Free Profile 1.8 Audio Gateway, so a headset's microphone works in
calls.
It publishes the Hands-Free Audio Gateway record on RFCOMM channel 13 with
wide band speech, adds the Audio and Telephony bits to the Class of Device,
and listens on that channel for any paired device.
A paired headset whose service records list Hands-Free and whose
.Ql Audio
switch is on gets the service level connection from
.Nm
three seconds after its link comes up, if it has not opened it itself.
There are no calls: the indicators report service and no call, and the AT
commands a headset sends to set up the connection, report its battery
(Apple's
.Ql AT+XAPL
and
.Ql AT+IPHONEACCEV )
or set its gains are answered; anything else gets
.Ql ERROR .
.Pp
The microphone is opened with
.Ql bluetooth audio Ar device Cm hfp ,
or when the headset asks for audio
.Pq Ql AT+BCC , ATA
or opens a SCO link itself.
With codec negotiation on both sides
.Nm
offers mSBC (wide band, 16 kHz) when the headset lists it, else CVSD (8 kHz),
and then opens an eSCO link, trying the HFP parameter sets in order
(T2 and T1 for mSBC; S4, S3, S1 and then D1 for CVSD).
An mSBC link the controller refuses falls back to CVSD for the session;
.Ql hfp cvsd
holds a headset to CVSD.
The link is carried through
.Pa /dev/btscoN ,
a device
.Nm
serves through
.Xr cuse 3
in the headset mixer's own format, and the mixer records from and plays to
it: the same
.Pa /dev/dsp.btN
(and
.Pa /dev/dsp ,
when the headset owns it) gains the headset's microphone.
The microphone closes with
.Ql bluetooth audio Ar device Cm a2dp ,
when the headset hangs up
.Pq Ql AT+CHUP
or closes the link, and with the connection; the mixer then goes back to
A2DP.
Opening a device for recording does not by itself switch a headset to HFP:
.Xr virtual_oss 8
tells nobody when a program starts recording.
A headset with no A2DP mixer (one without A2DP, or with its
.Ql Audio
switch off) gets no microphone.
.Ss Profile switching
A device's audio is carried by one profile at a time.
The hands-free gateway moves it to HFP by pointing the mixer's record and
play sides at
.Pa /dev/btscoN
through its control device, which closes the A2DP stream while
.Pa /dev/dsp.btN
stays open for programs, and back again afterwards.
.Sh PAIRING STORE
Two files under
.Pa /Local/Library/Preferences/SystemConfiguration ,
both written through SCPreferences (temporary file and rename on every
commit):
.Bl -tag -width "org.nextbsd.Bluetooth.keys.plist"
.It Pa org.nextbsd.Bluetooth.plist
Mode 0644.
Policy and device metadata, readable by tools:
.Bl -tag -width "ControllerPowerState" -compact
.It Ql ControllerPowerState
1 on, 0 off; missing means on.
.It Ql DiscoverableState
Inquiry scan at power-up; default off.
.It Ql AutoConnect
Reconnect paired devices at power-up; default on.
.It Ql AutoSwitchAudio
A headset that connects takes
.Pa /dev/dsp ;
default on.
.It Ql A2DPSink
Be a Bluetooth speaker; default off.
.It Ql PairedDevices
One dictionary per device, keyed by its address
.Pq Ql aa-bb-cc-dd-ee-ff :
.Ql Name , AddressType , Transports , ClassOfDevice , Appearance , PnPID ,
.Ql Profiles , HIDDescriptor , HIDVirtualCable , HIDReconnectInitiate ,
.Ql HIDNormallyConnectable , HIDBootDevice , HIDCountryCode ,
.Ql HIDSessionEstablished , AutoConnect , Trusted , Audio , Paired ,
and
.Ql LastConnected .
The cached HID descriptor lets a paired keyboard reconnect with no service
query.
.El
.It Pa org.nextbsd.Bluetooth.keys.plist
Mode 0600.
The secrets:
.Ql LinkKeys
(Classic),
.Ql LongTermKeys ,
.Ql IdentityResolvingKeys
and
.Ql SignatureKeys
(LE), keyed by the same address, in HCI byte order.
.El
.Pp
.Sy Keys are stored in plaintext .
NextBSD has no keychain; this is the same debt
.Xr wland 8
carries for passphrases.
The keys have a file of their own, mode 0600 and root-owned, so that when a
keychain lands it replaces that one file.
Key bytes are never written to the log.
.Pp
A missing or corrupt file reads as empty;
.Nm
never fails to start because of its store.
.Sh DYNAMIC STORE
.Bl -tag -width "State:/Bluetooth/Adapter/<node>"
.It Ql State:/Bluetooth
The daemon:
.Ql State
.Po
.Ql Idle ,
.Ql Active
or
.Ql NoIORegistry
.Pc ,
.Ql Adapters ,
.Ql PairedDevices ,
and the policy keys above.
.It Ql State:/Bluetooth/Adapter/<node>
One controller:
.Ql Address , Name , Transport , VendorID , ProductID , State , Powered ,
.Ql Discoverable , Connectable
and
.Ql Scanning .
.It Ql State:/Bluetooth/Device/<addr>
One paired or connected device:
.Ql Address , Name , ClassOfDevice , Adapter , Paired , Connected ,
.Ql Connecting , Profiles
(what runs over the link now, such as
.Ql HID )
and
.Ql RSSI
while connected.
A device that is neither paired nor connected has no key.
.It Ql State:/Bluetooth/Pairing/<addr>
A pairing prompt waiting for the user.
.It Ql State:/Bluetooth/Firmware/<ugen>
A firmware download.
.It Ql State:/Bluetooth/Device/<addr>/Audio
A device's audio while it has any:
.Ql Role
.Pq Ql Source : we play to it; Ql Sink : it plays to us ,
.Ql Route
.Pq Ql A2DP | HFP ,
.Ql Active , Codec
.Pq Ql SBC ; on HFP Ql CVSD | mSBC ,
.Ql Node , DefaultOutput , AVRCP ,
.Ql Volume
(0\(en127),
.Ql HandsFree
(the HFP connection is up),
.Ql Microphone
(its audio link is up) and
.Ql Battery
(percent, while the headset reports it).
.It Ql State:/Bluetooth/Adapter/<node>/A2DPSink
.Ql Enabled ,
and
.Ql Peer
while a phone plays to us.
.El
.Pp
Each change is announced with
.Xr notify_post 3
on
.Ql org.nextbsd.bluetooth.adapter ,
.Ql .scan ,
.Ql .pairing ,
.Ql .device ,
.Ql .firmware
or
.Ql .audio .
.Pp
Lists do not go in the store; they are served over the Mach service.
.Xr scutil 8
shows these keys.
.Sh CONTROLLER DIAGNOSTICS
The diagnostics of
.Xr bluetooth 8
run here and come back over the Mach service, so no separate HCI or L2CAP
tool is installed:
.Bl -tag -width "bluetooth ping"
.It Ql bluetooth info
reads the controller's version
.Pq Read_Local_Version_Information ,
features, ACL, SCO and LE buffer sizes, the HCI node's packet counters and
free credits, and its open links.
.It Ql bluetooth hci
sends one raw HCI command and returns the Command Complete or Command
Status that answers it, within 5 seconds.
Only root may; the caller's audit token is checked.
.Nm
is not told what such a command changed.
.It Ql bluetooth ping
sends one L2CAP Echo Request per call to a Classic device, paging it if
needed, and times the response.
.El
.Pp
Each runs on its own socket, never the controller's own, and blocks only
the caller and the Mach service while it waits.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/Local/Library/Preferences/SystemConfiguration/" -compact
.It Pa /usr/sbin/bluetoothd
The daemon.
.It Pa /dev/ioregistry
The in-kernel IORegistry, where controllers appear as
.Ql IOBluetoothHostController .
.It Pa /System/Library/LaunchDaemons/org.nextbsd.bluetoothd.plist
.Xr launchd 8
job.
.It Pa /var/log/bluetoothd.stderr
Log.
.It Pa /dev/btsco0 , /dev/btsco1 , ...
A headset's hands-free audio link, for its mixer only (root, 0600).
.It Pa /Local/Library/Preferences/SystemConfiguration/
.Pa org.nextbsd.Bluetooth.plist
(policy and paired devices) and
.Pa org.nextbsd.Bluetooth.keys.plist
(plaintext keys).
.El
